WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.347 --> 00:00:01.902
(dramatic piano music)

00:00:01.902 --> 00:00:03.870
- Today we're exploring the deep ocean

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.370
and its rarely seen creatures.

00:00:10.629 --> 00:00:12.204
My grandfather Jacques Cousteau

00:00:12.204 --> 00:00:15.390
was always looking to chronicle
the wonders of the ocean,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.617
and the giant isopod is
one of the most fascinating

00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:20.284
and strange-looking.

00:00:21.475 --> 00:00:23.761
These crustaceans live thousands of feet

00:00:23.761 --> 00:00:25.352
beneath the ocean's surface,

00:00:25.352 --> 00:00:29.378
typically between 500 and 7,000 feet deep.

00:00:29.378 --> 00:00:31.861
They can grow up to two feet long,

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:34.479
but this one's just a baby.

00:00:34.479 --> 00:00:36.164
The reason they're so big

00:00:36.164 --> 00:00:38.695
is due to something--
- [Man With Hat] Sorry.

00:00:38.699 --> 00:00:42.460
- Is due to something
called sea gigantism.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.239
We don't know why this happens.

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:46.765
It might be due to deep ocean pressure,

00:00:46.765 --> 00:00:49.018
or to scarcity of food resources.

00:00:49.018 --> 00:00:52.054
Either way, these guys are huge.

00:00:52.054 --> 00:00:54.674
They're carnivores and they
have four sets of jaws,

00:00:54.674 --> 00:00:58.468
but can go for years without eating.

00:00:58.468 --> 00:01:00.247
Are you guys eating?

00:01:00.247 --> 00:01:02.419
You barely lasted an hour.

00:01:02.419 --> 00:01:04.339
One isopod in captivity in Japan

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:07.256
survived five years without eating.

00:01:08.503 --> 00:01:09.833
OK, give me a chip.

00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:13.666
(dramatic instrumental music)

